violence has erupted on the streets of  mexico city after protesters clashed  with police the demonstration was held  to commemorate the 53rd anniversary of a  massacre which saw student protesters  mowed down by bullets as they called for  democratic reforms  a palpable expression of anger  met with a show of force  what started as a peaceful march in  mexico city in commemoration of a 1968  massacre of student protesters descended  into violence  and though over 50 years have passed  since the massacre the demonstrators  here don't feel enough has changed  the slogan of trial and punishment for  those responsible for the massacre is  still valid  we're here to demand justice and  punishment for those responsible for the  repression of october the 2nd  response  on october the 2nd 1968 as mexico was  gearing up for its first olympic games  students filled the streets calling for  democratic reforms  security forces opened fire  the official death toll is between 30  and 40 that activists and witnesses say  up to hundreds were killed  for years the government has been  reluctant to investigate  but one member of the 1968 student  movement said protests will not stop  until those behind the massacre face  justice  we won't forget october the 2nd and the  struggle will continue no matter who is  in government we will continue to  denounce it we are the voice of those  who demand justice and we are the voice  of those who have the right to tell the  truth  until the events of that day are  officially acknowledged the memory of  the massacre will continue to haunt  mexico
 

